          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-fe6c3e255563\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.92,\"analysis\":\"The direct declaration strongly avows the speaker's love. The narration that his voice is “full of promise” gives substantial independent support to a relational undertaking as well as an assertion of feeling. Murmuring against Nikki's lips and immediately repeating the declaration convey intimacy and intensity, but do not show that the words function as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The exact content and strength of the implied promise are unspecified, so performative force is substantial but less certain than truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A male speaker directly tells Nikki “I love you” during an intimate exchange; the highlighted utterance is followed by a repeated and intensified declaration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The utterance is framed as an intimate avowal delivered against Nikki's lips. Its repetition (“I love you so much, Nikki”) reinforces the declaration and personal reassurance, while “his voice full of promise” presents it as carrying commitment-like relational force. Nothing in the supplied passage indicates deception, irony, quotation, or involuntary blurting.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you,” he murmured against her lips\",\"supports\":\"Direct address and the ordinary declarative form strongly support a truth-conditional avowal in an intimate setting.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“his voice full of promise”\",\"supports\":\"The narrator explicitly frames the delivery as promise-bearing, supporting performative or undertaking force beyond affection alone.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you so much, Nikki.”\",\"supports\":\"The immediate repetition and naming of the addressee reinforce the avowal and reassurance; absent reflex-like framing, they do not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is at relative position 0.995379 in Ania Cofield's Nikki's Touch.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration occurs very near the end of the work, but without wider textual knowledge this supplies only weak contextual support for a culminating relational affirmation.\",\"confidence\":0.72}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P naturally capture the avowal and its explicitly promise-laden relational force. Intimate, emotional delivery can remain contextual because the passage supplies no positive evidence of expressive/reflexive production, and no residual core force requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
